% Section 2: Background (compressed)

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{IE Ontology Structure.}
\label{sec:bg:ontology}
An IE ontology $\mathcal{O} = (\mathcal{E}, \mathcal{R}, \sigma)$ defines entity types $\mathcal{E}$, relation types $\mathcal{R}$, and a mapping $\sigma: \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E})$, where $\mathcal{P}(\cdot)$ denotes the power set, specifying valid (head type, tail type) pairs per relation.
A triple $(h, r, t)$ is \emph{type-valid} if $(\mathrm{type}(h), \mathrm{type}(t)) \in \sigma(r)$.
We quantify constraint restrictiveness via \emph{constraint tightness} $\kappa(r)$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:tightness}, \S\ref{sec:composition}): tighter constraints leave fewer valid type pairs per relation.
We define ontology constraints broadly to encompass both formal ontology specifications (e.g., the KAIROS event ontology with explicit role-entity type mappings) and empirically derived type-pair constraints extracted from annotated training data.
The latter applies when datasets lack a formal ontology but provide typed annotations (SciERC, DocRED); OntoCoDec compiles both sources into the same constraint representation without modification.
Empirical constraints achieve 99\%+ instance-level coverage on held-out data (SciERC 99.0\%, DocRED 99.4\%); uncovered type pairs trigger grammar-first fallback (\S\ref{sec:composition}), ensuring no valid triples are blocked.

\paragraph{Grammar-Constrained Decoding.}
\label{sec:bg:gcd}
Grammar-constrained decoding restricts each token $y_t$ to a mask $M_g \in \{0, 1\}^{|V|}$ derived from a target grammar.
XGrammar~\citep{dong2025xgrammar} compiles JSON Schemas into pushdown automata for efficient enforcement of structural constraints; Outlines~\citep{willard2023outlines} and SGLang~\citep{zheng2024sglang} offer similar capabilities.
However, these methods are limited to context-free constraints and cannot enforce cross-field semantic rules (e.g., ``if $r$ = \textsc{Feature-of}, then $\mathrm{type}(h) \in \{\textsc{Method}, \textsc{OtherScientificTerm}\}$''), which depend on previously generated field values.

\paragraph{Problem Statement.}
\label{sec:bg:problem}
Given $\mathcal{O}$ and an autoregressive LM $\mathcal{M}$, we seek constraints $\mathcal{C}$ ensuring: (a)~\textbf{structural validity}, i.e., well-formed JSON with $\mathcal{E}$/$\mathcal{R}$ membership; and (b)~\textbf{type validity}, i.e., $(\mathrm{type}(h), \mathrm{type}(t)) \in \sigma(r)$ for every triple.
Condition (a) is context-free; (b) is context-sensitive but \emph{prefix-decidable}: at the point where a type token is generated, all information needed (relation type, entity registry) is available in the decoded prefix.
This requires that each entity type name maps to a distinguishable token sequence (\S\ref{sec:dynamic-masking}).

% Runtime overhead (inline paragraph, no subsection header to save space)

\paragraph{Runtime overhead.}
\label{sec:runtime}
S1 overhead is $+$13.1\% on SciERC and $+$2.43\% on DocRED (amortized over longer sequences); under LoRA, $+$10.0\% with 37\% lower absolute latency.
Full constraint compilation (schema parsing + grammar build): one-time $<$0.5\,s across all tested scales (Appendix~\ref{app:scalability}).
Scaling benchmarks confirm constant overhead (2--3\%) up to 200 entity types and 1{,}000 relations (Appendix~\ref{app:scalability}).

\paragraph{Beam search compatibility.}
\label{sec:beam-results}
Under greedy S1, the grammar-first fallback resolves DR$\leftrightarrow$CF conflicts one token at a time; beam search ($k{=}4$) explores paths that satisfy both layers jointly and reduces DR and CF simultaneously.
With LoRA, beam S1 eliminates all four violation types on SciERC for both models and on DocRED for Llama (R-F1 44--47; Appendix~\ref{app:beam-search}).
Under zero-shot, beam reduces DR to 0\% for calibrated models on SciERC (551 docs) and a DocRED subset (50 docs), and CF substantially (9.90\%\,$\to$\,0.53\% Qwen; 6.56\%\,$\to$\,0.93\% Llama on SciERC); Mistral remains a boundary condition (Appendix~\ref{app:beam-search}).
Total latency overhead is 1.54$\times$ (beam 1.32$\times$ $+$ constraint 1.17$\times$; Table~\ref{tab:beam-latency}).

% exp_ids: 70b_scierc_unconstrained, 70b_scierc_constrained, 70b_docred_unconstrained, 70b_docred_constrained
\paragraph{Cross-scale generalizability.}
\label{sec:70b-results}
On Llama-3.1-70B (AWQ INT4), OntoCoDec achieves DR$\to$0\% on SciERC (R-F1 $-$0.03\,pp) and \textbf{0\%} across ET, RV, and DR on the full DocRED 998-document set (R-F1 $+$0.04\,pp), one of three configurations to eliminate DR on DocRED (also achieved by LoRA + beam-4; Appendix~\ref{app:beam-search}).
CF rises to 7.01\% (from 0.07\%); the low baseline reflects under-measurement (malformed outputs bypass the checker), while constrained decoding exposes more triples to the consistency check (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
Model scale reduces but does not eliminate violations.

